WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
John: Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:04.330
I landed in Addis Ababa after a thirty hour
journey, met up with Bridget from the Gates

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:07.690
Foundation, and drove into the city where
I got some desperately needed and delicious

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:12.160
Ethiopian coffee, shopped for a bit at a local
market to get Henry this Ethiopia soccer jersey,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.140
and then went to visit Yekatit 12 Hospital.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.890
Hank, Ethiopia is one of the poorest countries
in the world.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:20.820
Most people live without electricity or running
water, most fields are tilled by animal-drawn

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.400
wooden plows, and the shortage of basic services
can be overwhelming.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.470
For example, the smallest hospital in my hometown
of Indianapolis has eight neonatologists.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.840
In Ethiopia, a nation of ninety million people,
there are seven.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.840
One of the first was Doctor Mulualem Gessesse.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.690
So, to get to the Yekatit 12 neonatal intensive
care unit, you walk on plywood over standing

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.140
water that smells of sewage.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.430
And then, up the stairs to the NICU.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.790
Doctor Gessesse and her nurses have to be
very inventive.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:49.510
For instance, they use the oxygen tanks, that
you see here, combined with tubing and water

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:52.770
to create a kind of C-Pap machine that keeps
premature babies' lungs inflated.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:56.579
They don't have respirators or real C-Pap
machines, which Doctor Gessesse cites as among

00:00:56.579 --> 00:00:58.070
her many critical needs.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:00.239
But still, she's been very successful.

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:03.629
In the past few years, her ward has gone from
treating a couple hundred babies per year

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:05.170
to more than two thousand.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.429
This little girl had to stay in the dark while
being treated.

00:01:07.429 --> 00:01:09.929
Dr Gessesse: We suspect tetanus because she
has spasms.

00:01:09.929 --> 00:01:12.889
I asked Doctor Gessesse several times about
her choice of specialty.

00:01:12.889 --> 00:01:14.689
Dr Gessesse: So that we continue 24 hours.

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:19.159
John: [speaking to Dr. Gessesse] So why do
you choose to do this then?

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:20.350
Eventually she answered me.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.390
Dr. Gessesse: My concern is for the Ethiopian
newborns.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:26.889
John: Hank, twenty years ago, more than fifteen
percent of children who were born in Ethiopia

00:01:26.889 --> 00:01:28.679
died before the age of five.

00:01:28.679 --> 00:01:30.640
Today, it's less than seven percent.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.779
Child mortality is falling faster in Ethiopia
than in the world overall, and it's much lower

00:01:34.779 --> 00:01:36.759
than it is in far richer countries.

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:37.759
How has this happened?

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:42.590
Well, I'm obviously no expert, but one, Ethiopia
is serious about health, and two, organizations

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:46.990
like the Gates foundation and Save the Children
are helping Ethiopians execute an Ethiopian

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:50.469
health strategy rather than trying to impose
a strategy upon them.

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:52.079
Like, take Kangaroo Mother Care.

00:01:52.079 --> 00:01:55.409
When preterm babies are well enough, they
live in the hospital for weeks or months with

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:58.219
their moms, getting constant skin-to-skin
contact.

00:01:58.219 --> 00:02:01.989
The babies and moms are together in the hospital
24/7, and studies have shown that this leads

00:02:01.989 --> 00:02:05.529
to better brain development, lower infection
rates, and better weight gain.

00:02:05.529 --> 00:02:09.129
It's cheap, it works, and when you make a
small investment in these babies' lives now,

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:12.599
they have a far better chance of growing up
to have healthy and productive adulthoods.

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:17.250
But Dr. Gessesse also told me stories of failed
outreach, as when a dozen C-Paps arrived from

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.980
a charity, all of them adult-sized, and none
of them of use to her.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.760
We have to listen to each other.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.510
Hank, I've found humans to be extraordinarily
generous within their social networks, like

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:29.810
think of how quickly we support friends and
colleagues in need.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.189
But lack of access to like, Tumblr and YouTube
makes most people living in absolute poverty

00:02:34.189 --> 00:02:39.010
totally voiceless in our online world, and
inevitably we begin to imagine their problems

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.750
as others, as things that don't happen to
us.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:47.060
If OUR kids were dying of malaria and diarrhea,
if dozens of OUR babies were dying for every

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.459
one that gets access to a jerry-rigged C-Pap
machine, we wouldn't stand for it!

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:54.079
But the thing is, these ARE our kids.

00:02:54.079 --> 00:02:57.569
OUR world is also VERY LITERALLY their world.

00:02:57.569 --> 00:03:00.670
Oh, and I did get to hang out with Bill Gates
who is brilliant, and I overcame my tremendous

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:04.290
fear of heights to fly in a helicopter with
him, and I will talk about all that stuff

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.290
in the coming weeks.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.049
But for now let us just pause to be grateful
to Dr. Gessesse, who has quietly and unassumingly

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:12.989
saved the lives of thousands of babies.

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:13.989
Our babies.

00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:15.870
As she told me, I have many children.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.590
Only two biological, but many children.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.230
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

